# Completion report: `endojs-endo-but-for-bots-ironhorse-panic-e2e-probe`

I opened draft PR **https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/pull/1372** (head `db4c6fd77b`, branch `llm-ironhorse-panic-e2e-probe`, base `llm-1706e63`). It is a probe, so it stays draft, its body carries `Refs: #1018`, and no gauntlet follows. The gap report is in the PR body and in a PR comment (https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/pull/1372#issuecomment-5887121928), where the later legs and the mentat job can read it.

## What I did
- I added one file, `rust/endo/xsnap/tests/panic_e2e_probe.rs`. It drives a real C-XS `Machine` worker (the real guarded `host_send_frame`, a recording transport, and a test-only callback that panics inside the FFI guard) through each stage of the design's pipeline. It covers three panic sources: an FFI callback panic, a JS stack overflow, and a meter abort.
- `cargo test --test panic_e2e_probe`: 5 passed, 6 ignored. The 6 ignored tests are pending cases, each naming the missing mechanism and the leg that owns it; they fail as intended under `--ignored`.
- The mentat job had not reported, so I followed the design's recorded leanings. I listed those three decisions in the PR body as review items: `Decode`/`StepLimit` count as panics, an uncaught throw terminates the worker, and the first transcript backend is the XS/CAS one.

## What exists today, stage by stage
1. **Panic mid-delivery:** partly.
   - Stack overflow and meter abort really are uncatchable: XS aborts with exit codes 2 and 7, and the enclosing `catch` never runs.
   - An FFI callback panic is confined to its worker, but the guest keeps running past the panic site.
2. **One `Panicked` outcome the supervisor can see:** missing. The live loop reports every XS abort as a metering `"terminated"`, so a stack overflow is billed as a metering death. An FFI panic reaches the supervisor by a different route (`XsnapError::Panicked`).
3. **Discarding the crank's outbound messages:** missing. The message sent before the panic escapes for all three sources.
4. **Terminate:** exists, through `unregister`.
5. **Snapshot restore:** the engine primitive exists, but nothing decides when to take snapshots.
6. **Replay up to the panicking delivery:** the transcript and replay mode are missing. Restoring the snapshot and re-delivering the committed deliveries by hand reproduces the exact pre-panic heap for all three sources, but every message is sent again.
7. **Debugger stop at the panic site:** missing; nothing emits a `<panic>` message.

## Gaps (12) and which leg owns each
| Gap | Owner |
|---|---|
| 1. FFI panic lets the guest run on past the panic site | cxs-panicked-adapter (needs a design decision first) |
| 2. Every XS abort is reported as a metering termination | cxs-panicked-adapter |
| 3. XS has seven recoverable abort exits but the design classifies two; the design's table also omits `ReentryLimit` and `HeapExhausted` | mentat amendment, then cxs-panicked-adapter and classification-lint |
| 4. No holding back of outbound messages | outbound-embargo |
| 5. "Slot Machine" has no code home (`packages/slots` does not exist) | **unowned**; a design question for the mentat |
| 6. No transcript; one crank can consume several inbound envelopes, which the design's crank definition doesn't cover | transcript, plus a mentat amendment |
| 7. No replay mode that suppresses re-sent messages | retry-replay |
| 8. None of the host callbacks are classified for replay | host-call-transcript |
| 9. No restore point while native handles are open | host-call-transcript and retry-replay |
| 10. No `<panic>` message and no hook where XS aborts | debugger-panic-break |
| 11. What an uncaught throw should do was not probed | mentat, then retry-replay |
| 12. The Ironhorse engine is not on the delivery path, so this probe covers C-XS only | **unowned**; the `-e ironhorse` stage 8/9 integration |

Gaps 1, 3, 5 and 6 need design decisions before the build legs proceed. Gaps 2, 4, 7, 8, 9 and 10 can be settled inside their owning legs, and gaps 11 and 12 need broader review.

